// winmm_enum_probe.nvgt - which winmm function is the one that kills the run?
//
// ANSWERED, run 37022466097: (a), the enumeration object alone. This script
// counted ports, called midi_output_port_count() FIRST and midi_output_port_name()
// through it, and died with stdout empty - ENUM_BEGIN itself never reached the
// file - so no MidiOutWinMM was ever constructed and winmm was never introduced.
// The tower below the fault is a virtual dispatch into an image Windows has
// already unmapped, which is (a) stated exactly. What cdb actually printed is
// only offsets - nvmidi!midi_output_port_count+0xc2 over
// nvmidi!midi_export_backend_byte+0x11bec / +0x11aa3 / +0x118df - so the named
// frame (getPortName, and MidiOutWinMM::openPort after it) is inferred from
// which source lines those are, not symbolicated. Open the port and the same
// vtable goes on to the backend's open path and its output thread, which is why
// the e2e script adds nothing to the failure and only runs longer before it.
//
// The crash is now an address, not a suspicion:
//
//   ExceptionAddress: 00007ffe991015e6 (<Unloaded_nvmidi.dll>+0x15e6)
//   ExceptionCode:    c0000005, Parameter[0] 8 = EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT
//   nvmidi!midi_output_port_count+0xc2  <- frame just under the fault
//
// The address is inside a library that has already been unloaded, so something
// is still holding code in that image. Two things in this repo can do that and
// they are told apart by which call has to happen first:
//
//   a) the enumeration object. enum_port<RtMidiOut>() builds one RtMidiOut and
//      keeps it for the life of the process; every port count, port name and
//      open goes through it. RtMidiOut is a class with virtual functions, so
//      its vtable lives in the plugin image, and if that object outlives the
//      script engine's teardown windows releases the page it points at, the
//      next virtual call is a jump into freed memory.
//   b) MidiOutWinMM. Opening a port constructs the winmm backend, whose
//      destructor and full teardown live in the same image. winmm also owns
//      an output thread, and midiOutClose() lets it run to the end of its
//      buffer before it returns - a thread that can still be inside the image
//      just after the image is gone.
//
// The two differ in ordering, and that is what this script reads: it counts
// ports first and opens last. A (a) alone dies inside the first block; (b)
// alone survives the count and dies in the second. If the first block is clean
// and the second is not, the print below the count stays in the log and the
// verdict is unambiguous.
//
// It also prints nothing derived from a port name, so a device-less runner
// still exercises everything but the naming.
//
// The pragma loads the library, and the trouble is in what the library does
// with winmm, so the pragma is the point rather than boilerplate.
#pragma plugin nvmidi

void main() {
	print("ENUM_BEGIN");

	// Block one: enumeration only. Builds the process-wide RtMidiOut on first
	// use, calls its virtual getPortCount(), and leaves without opening
	// anything - so no MidiOutWinMM is ever constructed on this path.
	const uint outputs = midi_output_port_count();
	print("ENUM_OUTPUTS " + outputs);
	const uint inputs = midi_input_port_count();
	print("ENUM_INPUTS " + inputs);

	// The line above this one is the boundary. Everything below needs winmm
	// to have been brought in, and if the fault is in MidiOutWinMM it is here
	// that the process stops rather than after the earlier count.
	const uint api_bytes = midi_api_name_byte_count();
	print("ENUM_API_BYTES " + api_bytes);
	print("ENUM_MIDDLE");   // the boundary marker, read against the exit code

	// Block two: winmm. Constructed by creating the port object; the ctor
	// inside RtMidiOut is what picks MidiOutWinMM for this backend.
	midi_output@ port_out = midi_output_create();
	print("ENUM_CREATED");
	const bool opened = port_out.open(0, "probe");
	print("ENUM_OPENED " + opened);
	port_out.close();
	print("ENUM_CLOSED");

	print("ENUM_END");
}
